      "created_at": "2023-12-06T17:43:06Z",
      "updated_at": "2023-12-06T17:43:06Z",
      "body": "> I'm actually a bit surprised that you haven't had to rewrite the whole libcrypto<->provider interface, or libcrypto's use of it. After all, all provided functions are being declared as `(void (*)(void))` by the `OSSL_DISPATCH` structure, and are then cast to whatever function signature they really have when populating the diverse method structures in libcrypto. That would be a huge potential case for a need of thunking, no? Or have I misunderstood the issue?\r\n\r\nThere should be no problem with casting things to `(void (*)(void))` when you pass the function pointer around. The ubsan errors are signaled only when the function signature as cast at the caller and as really implemented differs.\r\n",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
