      "updated_at": "2023-12-06T20:45:48Z",
      "body": "@t8m yes, thats exactly the problem, and those \"errors\" exist everywhere, because we currently make the assumption that the compilers we use will convert pointers to void to be the type specified in the implementation.   The nature of this problem is that, while every compiler we use does what we expect it to, its strictly speaking undefined behavior, which is what ubsan is warning about.  While we don't see an issue with this in production code, we apparently do see an issue in CFI, which relies on the fact that strictly spec-compliant C code explicitly matches the types of the passed parameters to a function pointer to the types listed in the target functions signature.  So to fix it we need to either have a per-type thunking layer that does the appropriate cast of a 'generic' function pointer to the correct type of the target pointer.  We can do that in some cases with a jump table possibly as you have described, but in many cases (for example the lhash code, or the stack_of code), we have to define a per type thunking function to pass around to do the reverse cast.\r\n\r\nAs I said in one of these earlier comments, regardless of the solution, yuk.",
